  "image_name": "501ba2d9a383c8a2b2d313a3aa025ab1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a conceptual sequence for how mineral-associated organic matter (MOAs) in soil is broken down and rebuilt. On the left, “original MOAs” are shown as organic matter attached to different minerals, with protection occurring through both chemical interactions and physical protection. The minerals include clay, iron oxides, and larger silicate particles such as quartz or feldspar.\n\nIn the middle, these original associations are transformed during microbial processing. Microorganisms and mineral surfaces interact with the old organic matter, producing transformed organic matter and microbial necromass. Between stages, Fe redox cycling is highlighted, with iron switching between Fe(III) and Fe(II), suggesting that changing iron oxidation state helps destabilize old associations and alter mineral-organic interactions.\n\nOn the right, new MOAs form when the newly produced organic matter and microbial residues become protected again on mineral surfaces. The diagram emphasizes that both transformed organic matter and necromass can become stabilized, creating “newly formed OM” associated with minerals.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a dynamic soil carbon cycle: mineral-bound organic matter is not static, but continually decomposed, microbially reworked, and reprotected through mineral interactions and iron redox processes.",
